            <fr:anchor>1391</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">sss-0023</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>sss-0023.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Finite Cardinal Arithmetic in a Topos">Finite Cardinal Arithmetic in a Topos</fr:title>
            <fr:meta name="external">
              <fr:link href="johnstone-2002.xml" title="Sketches of an Elephant: A Topos Theory Compendium" addr="johnstone-2002" type="local">[johnstone-2002]</fr:link>
            </fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p>In a topos with a natural numbers object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(N, z , s)]]></fr:tex>, you can define finite cardinals as objects that arise as the pullback along a morphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p : \top  \to  N]]></fr:tex> of the generic finite cardinal. Write <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[p]]]></fr:tex> for the cardinal corresponding to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
            <fr:p>The above characterization is a little obtuse and does warrant some more explanation. One way to make it more concrete is that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[z] = \bot ]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[s \circ  n] = \top  \oplus  [n]]]></fr:tex>, and that finite cardinals warrant a nice induction principle. If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> is a property expressible in the internal language such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\bot ]]></fr:tex> satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>, and that whenever <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top  \oplus  A]]></fr:tex> satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>; then every finite cardinal satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>. That is, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> forms a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(z,s)]]></fr:tex>-closed subobject of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex> and thus <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> is all of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
            <fr:p>My working mental model is in a presheaf topos, where the natural numbers object can be defined explicity as <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Delta (\mathbb {N})]]></fr:tex> .</fr:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:anchor>513</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">sss-0024</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>sss-0024.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Constant Presheaf">Constant Presheaf</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>The <fr:em>constant</fr:em> presheaf of a set <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> on a category <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> is a functor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Delta (X) : C^{op} \to  \mathbf {Set}]]></fr:tex> such that</fr:p>
                <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\Delta (X)c \colonequals  X]]></fr:tex>
                <fr:p>I often call this <fr:em>the discrete presheaf for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex></fr:em>, but I don't know if that's standard.</fr:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:p>The finite cardinals with respect to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Delta (\mathbb {N})]]></fr:tex> can be characterized then as,</fr:p>
            <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [0] \colonequals  \bot  ]]></fr:tex>
            <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [\mathsf {suc}(n)] \colonequals  \top  \oplus  [n] ]]></fr:tex>
            <fr:p>These obey nice algebraic properties.</fr:p>
            <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [nm] \cong  [n] \times  [m] ]]></fr:tex>
            <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [n + m] \cong  [n] \oplus  [m] ]]></fr:tex>
            <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [n^{m}] \cong  [m] \to  [n] ]]></fr:tex>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
